     found, and it must win. -->
<link rel="stylesheet" href="zs-fixes.css">
<style>
  /* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     THE MENU AND THE CHROME — 2026-08-18
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

     WHY THIS BLOCK IS HERE AND NOT IN THE <style> AT THE TOP OF THIS FILE.
     Six stylesheets load AFTER that block (zs-brand, zs-anim, zs-micro,
     zs-kit, zs-fixes, plus the two injected at runtime). Every rule below
     re-decides something one of them already decided, so at equal
     specificity every one of them would win and this pass would look
     exactly like the last five: correct values, defeated by source order.
     Owning the last word is the whole point of the slot.

     WHAT IT OWNS, and nothing else — the three surfaces the menu-and-chrome
     lane was given:
        1. div.phead / .appbar   the chrome that was louder than the screen
        2. #edmenu               the editor menu, screen 5 of the audit
        3. #sheetwrap            sheet chrome: every chooser in the app

     MEASURED, before → after. Real Chrome at 375×812 against the live board,
     screenshots decoded to pixels; the two builds differ by THIS EDIT ALONE
     (the before tree is the working tree with these three changes lifted
     out, so a sibling lane's in-flight work cannot be read as this one's):

        EDITOR MENU   largest flat colour   30.9% → 12.5%
                      pixels with chroma    15.7% → 67.6%   mean 13.3 → 43.8
                      solid ion             0.05% → 8.72%
                      solid role fill above the fold   NONE → 347×124 primary
                      dead ground at the foot          227px → 21px
                      most prominent painted things:
                        div.item 41.7% · #editor 39.4% · div.phead 8.8%
                        → div.item 48.0% · #editor 19.0% · div.item.lead 13.3%
                      header band vs content band, mean luminance:
                        1.21 → 0.20  (the header WAS brighter than the whole
                        screen under it; now the screen is five times it)

        THE HEADER    same header/content ratio on the rest of the app:
                      Keybinds .45 → .42 · Global .72 → .69 · HOME's app bar
                      .13 → .11 · any sheet .26 → .04.

        CHOOSER SHEET largest flat colour 6.8% → 3.0% · ion 0.51% → 4.35% ·
                      solid role fill NONE → the current row, 341×64.
        BOARD SHEET   largest flat colour 8.1% → 3.9% · ion 0% → 3.53% ·
                      solid role fill NONE → Check again, 341×52.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

  /* ── 1. THE CHROME RECEDES ─────────────────────────────────────────────
     div.phead was the most prominent painted element on 22 of 31 screens:
     the loudest thing in two-thirds of an accessibility product was the way
     OUT of the screen you had just chosen. It got there honestly — zs-brand
     paints it a raised glass gradient and zs-kit adds the lit top edge that
     makes glass read as glass — but the sum is a header that outranks its
     own content.

     It is quieted by DROPPING IT TO THE GROUND, not by hiding it: the fill
     becomes the page colour at 88% rather than a lighter slab, and the lit
     edge comes off. What stays: the blur, the fade under it, the room rail
     zs-fixes hangs off data-tint (that rule is #-specific and deliberately
     not touched here), the 16px title, the 44px back target, its border,
     its chevron, its word, its position in the tab order and every one of
     its aria attributes. Nothing about finding "back" changed except that
     it is no longer the brightest object on the screen. */
  div.appbar, div.phead {
    background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--bar-a), var(--bar-b));
    box-shadow: none;
  }
  /* --muted on this ground measures 7.7:1 (it was measured on the LIGHTER
     header before, so quieting the bar made the back label MORE legible, not
     less) — past AA for the 14.5px label,
     and the 1px --zs-edge box (3.26:1) is what says "control". The ink was
     --accent, i.e. the colour that is supposed to mean "press this and the
     thing happens" spent on brand furniture that leaves. On the five tinted
     detail screens zs-fixes re-colours this to the room's hue at far higher
     specificity, and that still wins — as it should. */
  .phead .pback {
    background-image: none;
    background-color: transparent;
    box-shadow: none;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .phead .pback:active, .phead .pback.zs-pressing {
    background-color: var(--panel2);
    color: var(--text);
  }
  /* The two accessibility signals zs-brand honours must keep winning: this
     block loads after it, so they are re-stated rather than left to lose.
     Reduced transparency ⇒ solid, no blur. More contrast ⇒ a real boundary
     and a back control with a visible face again. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-transparency: reduce) {
    div.appbar, div.phead { background-image: none; background-color: var(--panel); }
  }
  @media (prefers-contrast: more) {
    div.appbar, div.phead { background-image: none; background-color: var(--bg); }
    .phead .pback { background-color: var(--panel2); color: var(--text); }
  }

  /* ── 2. THE SECTION LIST — ONE CARD, FIVE ROOMS ────────────────────────
     THE PREVIOUS PASS OF THIS SECTION IS THE DEFECT THIS ONE FIXES, and it
     is worth saying plainly rather than quietly deleting: it took a screen
     measured as "five identical slabs with a coloured pinhead" and answered
     with five SATURATED CARDS — a per-room card fill, a per-room 4px rail, a
     solid per-room icon slab, a per-room chevron, and the first row promoted
     to the app's primary button face. It moved every number it set out to
     move. The owner then looked at it and said "it's a cluttered confusing
     mess", and he is right: a five-row table of contents in five hues, where
     row one inverts text polarity and wears the CTA face for a screen that
     has no action, is a toybox. Chroma was never the goal — LEGIBILITY was,
     and five competing signals are worth less than one.

     WHAT A ROOM COLOUR IS FOR HERE: telling five rows apart at a glance.
     That needs a cue, not a costume. So the colour retreats to exactly two
     places on the row — a 3px left edge and the glyph inside its own tinted
     well (the `.item .ico.ico-*` treatment this file has shipped since
     2026-08-17, which this block no longer overrides) — and everything else
     is the SAME card on all five rows: same fill, same sheen, same ink, same
     height, same chevron. Colour is still never the only signal: every row
     says its name and wears its own SHAPE, so the screen works with the hue
     removed entirely.

     NO ROW WEARS THE PRIMARY FACE. The old note argued that the screen's
     "first move" should be a primary because it had no action of its own —
     but a table of contents has five equal doors, and painting one of them
     like a button says the other four are lesser. Nothing here is an action,
     so nothing here is a cap. The one place the accent still means "press
     this and something happens" is a real button, and that is the point.

     SCREEN ASSIST STOPS BEING OVERVIEW'S COLOUR. Both rooms resolved to
     #6f9bff, so five rooms had four hues and "the blue one" identified
     nothing. Overview keeps the accent (it is the config's front door);
     Screen Assist takes cyan #5ccfd6 — free now that the primary cap is
     blue (see :root --cap-*), not a family colour, and not the gradient it
     used to wear, which was a sixth icon treatment in a five-item list.
       glyph on its own 15% well, against the card:
         accent 6.61:1 · gold 9.13:1 · violet 5.30:1 · orange 8.14:1
         cyan 9.60:1 — AA on all five, and the 45% edge clears 1.4.11.

     THE ROWS GOT SHORTER, WHICH IS THE SECOND HALF OF THE FIX. At 112-124px
     with a two-line description each, the list was 788px in a 676px viewport
     — a five-item menu that did not fit on one screen, with the fifth room
     below the fold behind the device bar. The descriptions are cut to a few
     words or dropped where the title already says it (SECTIONS, above), so
     the row is 68px and all five are visible at once. */
  #edmenu .item { --room: var(--room-plain); }
  #edmenu .item[data-room="overview"] { --room: var(--accent, #6f9bff); }
  #edmenu .item[data-room="aim"]      { --room: var(--gold); }
  #edmenu .item[data-room="behavior"],
  #edmenu .item[data-room="translation"] { --room: var(--gold); }   /* D163/D164: Aim's gold */
  #edmenu .item[data-room="bindings"] { --room: var(--purple); }
  #edmenu .item[data-room="smart"]    { --room: var(--rose, #f47fa4); }   /* Auto Actions' rose, D127 */
  #edmenu .item[data-room="vision"]   { --room: var(--tint-vision, #5ccfd6); }
  /* D215: EXO Studio wears Screen Assist's cyan (a Core draws no Screen
     Assist door; computer vision is the subject of both), Legacy Devices the
     GPC room's bolt orange */
  #edmenu .item[data-room="studio"]   { --room: var(--tint-vision, #5ccfd6); }
  #edmenu .item[data-room="legacy"]   { --room: var(--tint-gpc, #ff9457); }

  /* ONE CARD, STATED ONCE. Longhand borders only: the `border` shorthand
     would reset the rail to 1px the moment anything set border-left-color
     (DESIGN.md rule 7). The fill is the app's ordinary card — the same
     surface every other list row in the product uses — so the section list
     stops being a place with its own visual language. */
  #edmenu .item {
    border-left-style: solid;
    border-left-width: 3px;
    border-left-color: var(--room);
    /* 80/11 is not a taste call, it is the narrowest phone this ships to.
       Measured at 380x800: the fifth room's foot lands at y669 and the
       device bar starts at y721 — 53px of clearance, so the whole table of
       contents is on one screen with nothing hidden behind the bar. The
       version this replaces was 788px of rows in a 676px viewport at the
       WIDER 420px frame, i.e. it did not fit on any phone. */
    /* D202: D201's 64 px full-bleed doors are undone (the owner wanted the
       picture tiles back); the doors are these again. */
    min-height: 80px;
    margin: 11px 14px;
    padding: 13px 15px;
    background-color: var(--panel);
    background-image: linear-gradient(180deg,
      var(--zs2-card-a, rgba(255,255,255,.075)), var(--zs2-card-b, rgba(255,255,255,.03)));
  }
  /* GEOMETRY ONLY on the chip — the hue comes from `.item .ico.ico-*` above,
     which is the same tinted-glyph treatment HOME's device rows wear. The
     old rule here painted it SOLID in the room's hue, which is what turned
     five quiet cues into five colour blocks. 40px still clears the scanning
     job at a 68px row. */
  #edmenu .item .ico { width: 40px; height: 40px; }
  #edmenu .item .ico svg { width: 21px; height: 21px; }
  #edmenu .item .ico img.ico3d { width: 26px; height: 26px; }
  /* THE CHEVRON IS NOT A CUE. Five coloured arrows saying "this row is a
     door" when all five are doors is decoration wearing a signal's clothes.
     Muted measures 6.0:1 on a card — legible, and silent. */
  #edmenu .item .chev { color: var(--muted); font-size: 19px; }
  /* a row whose title already says everything renders no hint at all rather
     than an empty line that still takes vertical space */
  #edmenu .item .hint:empty { display: none; }

  /* PRESS FEEDBACK. One state for all five rows, like everything else here:
     the app's control gradient over the card, and the rail brightens. */
  #edmenu .item:active, #edmenu .item.zs-pressing {
    background-image: linear-gradient(180deg,
      var(--zs2-ctl-a, rgba(255,255,255,.145)), var(--zs2-ctl-b, rgba(255,255,255,.065)));
    border-left-color: var(--room);
  }
  /* the focus ring stays ONE colour app-wide — zs-kit §17 forbids tinting it,
     and that is an accessibility decision, not a style one. Only the offset
     is restated, because the rail would otherwise swallow the ring's left
     edge on the row it is drawn against. */
  #edmenu .item:focus-visible { outline-offset: -3px; }
  /* zs-kit's `body.a11y-big .item{min-height:82px}` is (0,2,0) and this
     block is (1,1,0), so the bigger-targets switch would have stopped
     reaching these rows the moment the height moved here. Re-stated so it
     still does what it says on the one screen every room is entered through. */
  body.a11y-big #edmenu .item { min-height: 96px; }
  /* D202: a door wearing a picture. The picture is the row's full height
     and flush to its left edge; the row's own border and corners are the
     picture's edge, so the room rail gives way to the ordinary 1 px edge
     there (the picture IS the cue). 80 px, the icon doors' height, so every
     door is one size. */
  #edmenu .item.has-pic { padding: 0 15px 0 0; align-items: stretch;
    overflow: hidden; border-left-width: 1px; border-left-color: var(--line); }
  #edmenu .item.has-pic .ico.ico-img { width: 80px; height: auto; min-height: 80px;
    flex: 0 0 80px; align-self: stretch; }
  #edmenu .item.has-pic .t { align-self: center; padding: 10px 0; }
  #edmenu .item.has-pic .chev { align-self: center; }
  body.a11y-big #edmenu .item.has-pic .ico.ico-img { width: 96px; flex-basis: 96px; min-height: 96px; }

  /* OWNER, 2026-10-02: (1) every picture BLENDS into its card on the right
     instead of ending on a hard line; (2) the settings (editor) menu never
     scrolls: the doors share the height, between 52 and 80 px each. */
  .hs-link .hd-pic, .hs-gun .gi, #edmenu .item.has-pic .ico.ico-img{
    -webkit-mask-image:linear-gradient(to right,#000 55%,transparent 100%);
            mask-image:linear-gradient(to right,#000 55%,transparent 100%)}
  #edmenu{overflow-y:hidden;display:flex;flex-direction:column}
  /* D206 (owner): EVERY DOOR IS EXACTLY ONE HEIGHT. The shared-height rule
     above still let a picture door (no padding) and an icon door (13 px
     padding) come out 65 and 80 px. Now each row IS 72 px, border-box, and
     the gap between rows is 12 px. Seven doors: 8 + 7 x 72 + 7 x 12 = 596
     px, plus the device bar's 112 px = 708 px - inside the 722 px a 380 x
     800 phone gives the menu and the exe's 774 px, so it never scrolls. */
  #edmenu>.item{box-sizing:border-box;flex:0 0 56px;height:56px;
    min-height:56px !important;max-height:56px;margin:6px 14px;
    padding-top:0;padding-bottom:0;overflow:hidden}
  #edmenu>.item .t{min-width:0}
  #edmenu>.item .lab,#edmenu>.item .hint{overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;white-space:nowrap}
  /* the picture tile: 80 px wide, the row's full 72 px */
  #edmenu .item.has-pic>.ico.ico-img{min-height:0 !important;height:56px;
    width:64px;flex:0 0 64px;align-self:stretch}
  /* Bigger Targets asks for bigger rows than seven can fit: there the
     menu scrolls rather than cut a door off */
  body.a11y-big #edmenu{overflow-y:auto}
  body.a11y-big #edmenu>.item{flex-basis:96px;height:96px;min-height:96px !important;max-height:96px}
  body.a11y-big #edmenu .item.has-pic>.ico.ico-img{height:96px;width:96px;flex-basis:96px}

  /* D207 (owner): "scale the images to where you can see the whole image".
     The door pictures and HOME's weapon picture are CONTAINED, not cropped,
     and sit against the tile's left edge, so the spare width falls on the
     right, under the blend mask above. The ground under each one is that
     picture's own black (sampled from its corners), so nothing reads as a
     bar where the picture ends. */
  #edmenu .item .ico.ico-img .ico-pic, .hs-gun .gi .gi-pic{object-fit:contain;object-position:left center}
  #edmenu .item[data-room="overview"] .ico.ico-img{background:#000}
  #edmenu .item[data-room="smart"] .ico.ico-img{background:#05040a}
  #edmenu .item[data-room="auto"] .ico.ico-img, .hs-gun .gi{background:#080415}
  /* THE WHOLE AI SCRIPTS PICTURE (owner, 2026-10-02: "the picture for the
     active weapon and AI Scripts is too zoomed in; zoom it out so I can see
     the whole picture at once"). zs-ai-scripts.png is now the owner's whole
     artwork - all three weapon panels and the EXO word - trimmed only of its
     empty border (480 x 287, 5:3), where it used to be a square cut from the
     middle. The weapon card's frame takes the picture's own shape (75 px
     tall x 126 wide, in the frame rule above), and on both the fade to the right starts late so the
     right-hand panel is not faded away. The door keeps D206's one 80 px
     frame like every other door, so the picture sits whole inside it. */
  #edmenu .item[data-room="auto"] .ico.ico-img, .hs-gun .gi{
    -webkit-mask-image:linear-gradient(to right,#000 88%,transparent 100%);
            mask-image:linear-gradient(to right,#000 88%,transparent 100%)}
  #edmenu .item[data-room="auto"] .ico.ico-img .ico-pic{object-position:center}
  #edmenu .item[data-room="aim"] .ico.ico-img{background:#000}   /* 2026-10-23: the owner's glowing crosshair, on black */
  #edmenu .item[data-room="translation"] .ico.ico-img{background:#000}
  #edmenu .item[data-room="bindings"] .ico.ico-img{background:#000}   /* D233: what you are holding */
  #edmenu .item[data-room="bindings"] .ico.ico-img .ico-pic{object-fit:cover;object-position:center}   /* D233: the owner's translation tile */
  /* D215: the two placeholder pictures' own grounds (brand/zs-exo-studio.svg,
     brand/zs-legacy-devices.svg), until the owner's artwork replaces them */
  #edmenu .item[data-room="studio"] .ico.ico-img{background:#0a1416}
  #edmenu .item[data-room="legacy"] .ico.ico-img{background:#000}

  /* D215: EIGHT DOORS ON A CORE (Device, Aim, Mappings, Scripts, AI Scripts,
     EXO Studio, Translation, Legacy Devices). Still 72 px each (D206), now
     8 px apart: 8 + 8 x 72 + 8 x 8 = 648 px, plus the device bar's 112 =
     760 px, inside the exe's 774, so the exe's menu never scrolls. A phone
     shorter than that (380 x 800 gives 722) scrolls the menu rather than
     cut the last door off. */
  #edmenu>.item{margin:4px 14px}
  #edmenu{overflow-y:auto}
  /* D229 (owner, 2026-10-07: "scale everything down so that page of
     sections isn't scrollable"): with D228's three group headings the doors
     are 56 px, 8 px apart, the headings 24 px. Eight doors (an EXO) =
     8 x 64 + 3 x 24 + 8 = 592 px, plus the device bar's 112 = 704 px:
     inside the exe's 774 AND a 380 x 800 phone's 722, so neither scrolls. */
  /* D230 (owner: "way too small ... make it as big as possible where it
     can fill up the screen without needing to scroll"): the doors SHARE the
     menu's height. Each grows from 56 px up to 104 px, so the list always
     fills the screen it is on and never scrolls; the picture tile follows
     the row's height. */
  #edmenu>.item{margin:4px 14px;flex:1 1 0;height:auto;min-height:56px !important;max-height:104px}
  #edmenu .item.has-pic>.ico.ico-img{height:auto;width:96px;flex:0 0 96px}
  #edmenu .item .lab{font-size:18px}
  #edmenu .item .hint{font-size:13.5px}

  /* D208 (owner) kept ~0.4 inch clear under the device card for the inputs
     HOME shows. Superseded 2026-10-02 (owner: "move the EXO Core lower and
     increase the profile image size to fill the dead space"): the presses
     now have a full row reserved in the foot (.hs-keys), so that gap was
     only dead space. The device card sits on the foot (its own 10px margin
     is the breathing room) and the picture, the one thing that flexes,
     takes the rest. */
  #home:has(.hs-art){padding-bottom:0}
  /* D215: "Weapon detection: on your PC (EXO Studio)" is the owner's whole
     sentence, so it wraps rather than lose "(EXO Studio)" to an ellipsis;
     D218's "Weapon detection: off in GPC mode" the same way */
  .hs-gun.is-pc .gn,.hs-gun.is-gpc .gn{white-space:normal;overflow:visible;text-overflow:clip;line-height:1.3}
  /* no game picture (owner, 2026-10-02: no EDID chip, so the app says what
     to set): the whole instruction shows, wrapped, behind an amber dot */
  .hs-gun.is-nosignal .gs{white-space:normal;overflow:visible;text-overflow:clip;
    line-height:1.3;align-items:flex-start}
  .hs-gun.is-nosignal .gd{background:var(--st-warn);margin-top:5px}
  /* ── 3. SHEET CHROME — EVERY CHOOSER IN THE APP ────────────────────────
     The config chooser was measured pixel-for-pixel unchanged, and so was
     every other sheet, because a sheet's look is not written by whatever
     opens it — it is this chrome. Two things were wrong with all of them at
     once: the scrim was the single most prominent painted element on every
     sheet in the app — 42.9% of the viewport on the board sheet, 61.1% on a
     chooser — in one flat tone, and the row that says "this is the one you
     have now" said it with a 1px border, which is why a chooser measured
     0.51% ion and had no solid role fill at all.

     The scrim becomes a gradient. It costs nothing, it dims the top of the
     screen harder than the bottom (so the sheet reads as lifted off the
     screen rather than pasted onto it), and the largest flat run on a
     chooser falls 6.8% → 3.0%. */
  #sheetwrap .scrim {
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--scrim-a), var(--scrim-b));
  }
  /* THE CURRENT CHOICE IS SOLID. A chooser exists to answer one question and
     the answer was a hairline. Ion here is not decoration: every row in a
     chooser is a control, and this is the one already in force. */
  #sheetwrap .pkr.cur {
    background-color: var(--btn-1-bg);
    background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--cap-a,#93b6ff), var(--cap-b,#6f9bff) 52%, var(--cap-c,#5b86e8));
    border-color: var(--btn-1-edge);
    color: var(--btn-1-ink);
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  #sheetwrap .pkr.cur .pki { color: var(--btn-1-ink); }
  #sheetwrap .pkr.cur .pkn em { color: var(--cap-ink-soft); }
  #sheetwrap .pkr.cur:active { background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--cap-c,#5b86e8), var(--cap-p,#4573d6)); }
  /* the grid picker's selected tile, same answer at tile size: it wore a 1px
     ion outline plus a 1px ion glow, which at 56px is four pixels of signal
     in a sheet of forty identical wells. */
  #sheetwrap .pk.cur {
    background-color: var(--btn-1-bg);
    background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--cap-a,#93b6ff), var(--cap-c,#5b86e8));
    border-color: var(--btn-1-edge);
    box-shadow: none;
    color: var(--btn-1-ink);
  }
  #sheetwrap .pk.cur .pklab { color: var(--cap-ink-soft); font-weight: 700; }

  /* ── 4. THE APP'S OWN CONTRAST SWITCH STILL WINS ───────────────────────
     Everything above is written at #-specificity so it can beat six later
     stylesheets — which means it beats zs-kit's `body.a11y-contrast` rules
     too, and those are not decoration. Left alone, turning the app's High
     Contrast switch ON would have DROPPED the fill out of the back control
     and left the gradients painting. They are re-stated here, higher, so the
     switch keeps doing what it says. This is the price of owning the last
     word in the cascade and it is paid explicitly rather than discovered. */
  body.a11y-contrast div.appbar, body.a11y-contrast div.phead {
    background-image: none; background-color: var(--bg);
  }
  body.a11y-contrast .phead .pback {
    background-color: var(--panel2); color: var(--text);
  }
  body.a11y-contrast #edmenu .item {
    background-image: none; box-shadow: none; border-color: var(--muted);
  }
  /* the promoted first row is gone (§2 above), so there is nothing left here
     to re-state for it: every row is one card in both modes now. */
  /* the rail is the one thing contrast mode should keep: it is a 5:1+
     boundary, which is exactly what this mode is asking for */
  body.a11y-contrast #edmenu .item { border-left-color: var(--room); }
  body.a11y-contrast #sheetwrap .pkr.cur, body.a11y-contrast #sheetwrap .pk.cur {
    background-image: none; background-color: var(--btn-1-bg);
    border-color: var(--btn-1-ink);
  }
</style>
<!-- Inside the ZeroStick mobile app (Capacitor) there is no Web Bluetooth;
     zs-ble-cap.js puts one in front of the native plugin. document.write is
     deliberate: it is the one way to load a script synchronously from a
     condition during parsing, so it is in place before zs-ble.js runs. The
     exe and every browser skip it (no window.Capacitor) and keep the real
     API, or the truthful "cannot" from zs-ble.js. -->
<script>
  if (window.Capacitor && window.Capacitor.isNativePlatform && window.Capacitor.isNativePlatform()) {
    document.write('<script src="zs-ble-cap.js"><\/script>');
  }
</script>
<!-- D423: the phone app shell's live updates (zerostick-app/www/zs-live-update.js; never on the exe) -->
<script>if(window.Capacitor&&window.Capacitor.isNativePlatform&&window.Capacitor.isNativePlatform()){document.write('<script src="/zs-live-update.js"><\/script>');}</script>
<script src="zs-ble.js"></script>
<!-- D335: the firmware's "changed" push (message id 0) on the USB line and on
     Bluetooth reaches ZS.onExternalChange. Here because this is the first
     point at which both transports (zs-serial.js, zs-ble.js) exist. -->
<script>if(window.wireChangePush) wireChangePush();</script>
<script>
  /* WHERE "YOUR DEVICES" GOES. On the desktop exe the selector is our own
     devices.html. Inside the ZeroStick mobile app (Capacitor) this same
     page is served from www/exo/, and since D264 (2026-10-10) the app's home
     IS this same devices.html beside it (www/index.html only forwards to
     exo/devices.html), so both lead to the list. ?pick=1 shows the list
     instead of re-opening the remembered device; the phone gets no ?app=1,
     which is the exe's window mode. Function declarations hoist, so the two
     call sites above resolve. */
  function devicesURL(){
    var cap = window.Capacitor;
    var native = !!(cap && cap.isNativePlatform && cap.isNativePlatform());
    if (native) return 'devices.html?pick=1';
    /* the exe's full-bleed mode rides along, so the picker and the way back
       into a product stay app-shaped (devices.html carries it forward) */
    return document.body.classList.contains('zs-app') ? 'devices.html?pick=1&app=1' : 'devices.html?pick=1';
  }
  /* ── Bluetooth mode ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Reached as phone.html?ble=1, which is where mobile.html sends a phone.

     It has to be THIS document rather than a page of its own, and the reason
     is not stylistic: a GATT connection belongs to the page that opened it and
     dies on navigation. A separate connect screen that then sent you to the
     app would drop the link on the way. So the gate lives over the app and
     lifts once a link exists.

     Everything downstream is untouched. zs-ble.js shims fetch('/agent/*'), so
     api(), the raw fetches in phone-sections.js and anything added later all
     run over the radio without knowing it. */
  (function () {
    'use strict';
    if (!/[?&]ble=1/.test(location.search)) { return; }
    /* D335: a host (EXO Studio) owns the connection and says its own
       "Connect your EXO"; this gate would only stand in front of it */
    if (window.ZSHOST && window.ZSHOST.hides('connectGate')) { return; }

    document.body.classList.add('zs-app');   /* full-bleed: no bezel, no notch */

    var gate = document.getElementById('blegate');
    if (!gate) { return; }
    /* a preview launch (zs-demo-agent.js is already answering): no gate */
    if (window.ZSDEMO && window.ZSDEMO.active()) { gate.hidden = true; return; }
    gate.hidden = false;

    function mark() {
      return window.ZSLOGO
        ? window.ZSLOGO.monogram({ size: 64, decorative: true })
        : '';
    }

    /* Developer preview (owner, 2026-10-12): "add a bypass button after i
       spam myself into dev mode ... so i can see the ui of the exo without
       needing to connect to it." Offered only once the hidden 10-tap unlock
       has set zs-exo-dev (D271), so a customer never sees it. It lifts the
       gate WITHOUT a link: the screens run on their local preview values,
       every /agent call fails as offline, and nothing reaches any box. */
    var previewing = false;
    function devUnlocked() {
      /* store builds (zs-build.js dev:false) have no developer door at all:
         Apple rejects hidden features (Guideline 2.3.1), see devices.html */
      if (window.ZS_BUILD && window.ZS_BUILD.dev === false) { return false; }
      if (window.ZSHOST && window.ZSHOST.hides('devPreview')) { return false; }   /* D335 */
      try { return localStorage.getItem('zs-exo-dev') === '1'; } catch (e) { return false; }
    }
    function bypassBtn() {
      return devUnlocked()
        ? '<button class="zs-gate-alt" id="blepreview">Preview without connecting</button>'
        : '';
    }
    function wireBypass() {
      var b = document.getElementById('blepreview');
      if (!b) { return; }
      b.addEventListener('click', function () {
        /* Owner, 2026-10-12: the preview should "fake as if its connected"
           with his profiles and a controller. zs-demo-agent.js answers
           /agent/* from a capture of his board; if it cannot start, the
           preview still opens, offline, rather than leaving him at the gate. */
        function lift(msg) {
          previewing = true;
          gate.hidden = true;
          try { if (window.PHONE && window.PHONE.toast) { window.PHONE.toast(msg); } }
          catch (e) { /* cosmetic */ }
        }
        b.disabled = true;
        if (!window.ZSDEMO) { lift('Preview — not connected. Changes stay on this phone.'); return; }
        window.ZSDEMO.available().then(function (has) {
          if (!(has && window.ZSDEMO.begin())) {
            lift('Preview — not connected. Changes stay on this phone.');
          }
        });
      });
    }

    function paintIntro(cap) {
      if (!cap.ok) {
        /* Say exactly what is wrong and what to do instead. "Connection
           failed" here would send someone to reset hardware that is fine. */
        gate.innerHTML =
          '<div class="zs-gate-mark">' + mark() + '</div>' +
          '<h2>' + (cap.reason === 'noadapter'
                    ? 'Bluetooth is off, or not fitted'
                    : 'This browser can’t use Bluetooth') + '</h2>' +
          '<p class="zs-gate-why">' + cap.message + '</p>' +
          '<p class="zs-gate-foot">The desktop app connects over USB and Wi‑Fi ' +
          'and has every feature this page does.</p>' +
          (cap.reason === 'noadapter'
            ? '<button class="zs-gate-alt" id="blerecheck">Check again</button>'
            : '') +
          bypassBtn();
        wireBypass();
        var again = document.getElementById('blerecheck');
        if (again) {
          again.addEventListener('click', function () {
            /* Bluetooth can be switched on without reloading, and making
               somebody reload to be believed is a bad answer. */
            window.ZSBLE.available().then(paintIntro);
          });
        }
        return;
      }
      /* A phone that has connected before goes straight back to ITS EXO
         (owner, 2026-10-13: "just put me into it, not a sub-menu to choose
         my EXO"): one button, no chooser, and a small way out for a second
         box. A first-time phone gets the chooser, as before. */
      var mine = window.ZSBLE.remembered ? window.ZSBLE.remembered() : null;
      gate.innerHTML =
        '<div class="zs-gate-mark">' + mark() + '</div>' +
        '<h2>Connect to your ZeroStick</h2>' +
        (mine
          ? '<p>Make sure your EXO is powered on and nearby.</p>'
          : '<p>Make sure the adapter is powered on. Your phone will ask which ' +
            'device to pair with — choose <b>ZeroStick EXO</b>.</p>') +
        '<button class="zs-gate-btn" id="blego">Connect</button>' +
        (mine ? '<button class="zs-gate-alt" id="bleother">Use a different EXO</button>' : '') +
        bypassBtn() +
        '<p class="zs-gate-foot" id="blenote"></p>';
      document.getElementById('blego').addEventListener('click', function () { go(); });
      var other = document.getElementById('bleother');
      if (other) {
        other.addEventListener('click', function () {
          window.ZSBLE.forget();
          paintIntro(cap);
          go();                      /* nothing remembered now: the chooser */
        });
      }
      wireBypass();
    }

    /* Opening the app with an EXO remembered: no button to press. If it is
       off or away, the normal screen comes back with a sentence saying so. */
    function paintAuto() {
      var mine = window.ZSBLE.remembered();
      gate.innerHTML =
        '<div class="zs-gate-mark">' + mark() + '</div>' +
        '<h2>Connecting to your EXO…</h2>' +
        '<p>' + (mine && mine.name ? mine.name : 'ZeroStick EXO') + '</p>' +
        '<p class="zs-gate-foot" id="blenote"></p>';
    }

    function go(opts) {
      var btn = document.getElementById('blego');
      var note = document.getElementById('blenote');
      if (btn) { btn.disabled = true; btn.textContent = 'Looking…'; }
      if (note) { note.textContent = ''; }
      return window.ZSBLE.connect(opts).then(function (info) {
        window.ZSBLE.installFetchShim('/agent');
        previewing = false;
        gate.hidden = true;
        /* Nudge the app to re-read the board now that there is a link. It
           polls anyway, but waiting up to 8s to show anything after a
           successful connect feels broken. */
        try {
          if (window.PHONE && window.PHONE.toast) {
            window.PHONE.toast('Connected to ' + (info.name || 'your ZeroStick'));
          }
        } catch (e) { /* cosmetic */ }
      }).catch(function (e) {
        if (!document.getElementById('blego')) { paintIntro(window.ZSBLE.supported()); }
        btn = document.getElementById('blego');
        note = document.getElementById('blenote');
        if (btn) { btn.disabled = false; btn.textContent = 'Try again'; }
        /* ZSBLE.explain turns the browser's developer-facing errors into
           something worth reading. Cancelling the chooser is not a failure
           and must not be dressed up as one. */
        if (note) { note.textContent = window.ZSBLE.explain(e); }
      });
    }

    /* If the link drops later, put the gate back rather than letting every
       control quietly fail — an adapter that has gone is exactly the thing the
       user needs told about. */
    window.ZSBLE.on('status', function (s) {
      /* zs-ble reconnects by itself after a drop: say so, and lift the gate
         again the moment it is back, with no tap needed */
      if (s.connected) {
        if (!gate.hidden && !previewing) { gate.hidden = true; }
        return;
      }
      if (gate.hidden && !previewing) {
        if (s.reconnecting) {
          paintAuto();
          var h = gate.querySelector('h2');
          if (h) { h.textContent = 'Reconnecting to your EXO…'; }
          var n = document.getElementById('blenote');
          if (n) { n.innerHTML = '<button class="zs-gate-alt" id="blestop">Stop and choose</button>'; }
          var stop = document.getElementById('blestop');
          if (stop) {
            stop.addEventListener('click', function () {
              window.ZSBLE.disconnect();
              paintIntro(window.ZSBLE.supported());
            });
          }
        } else {
          paintIntro(window.ZSBLE.supported());
        }
        gate.hidden = false;
      }
    });

    /* Ask both questions: can the BROWSER do it (sync), and does this DEVICE
       have a radio (async). Paint the sync answer first so the gate is never
       blank, then refine. */
    paintIntro(window.ZSBLE.supported());
    window.ZSBLE.available().then(function (cap) {
      paintIntro(cap);
      /* an EXO this phone has used before: connect now, no tap, no chooser */
      if (cap.ok && window.ZSBLE.remembered && window.ZSBLE.remembered()) {
        paintAuto();
        go({ picker: false });
      }
    });
  })();
</script>
<script>
  /* ── USB mode: the ZeroStick Core (D104) ───────────────────────────────
     Reached as phone.html?core=1, the picker's tile for the Core (?usb=1 is
     accepted for the transport alone). On a PC the Core is reached over
     Web Serial; the shim went in at the top of this file, before the app's
     first fetch, and this block only opens the port.

     It is the SAME gate element and the SAME classes as the Bluetooth block
     above, on purpose: it is the same moment (nothing attached yet, so every
     control under it would be lying about what it is wired to), and a port,
     like a GATT link, belongs to the page that opened it and dies on
     navigation, so the connect has to happen here over the app. A phone
     opening this page cannot do USB and is pointed at Bluetooth. The
     Bluetooth block above is untouched: the two never run on one page. */
  (function () {
    'use strict';
    if (!window.ZS_USB_PAGE || !window.ZSSERIAL) { return; }
    if (window.ZSHOST && window.ZSHOST.hides('connectGate')) { return; }   /* D335 */
    if (/[?&]ble=1/.test(location.search)) { return; }   /* Bluetooth's page */

    var gate = document.getElementById('blegate');
    if (!gate) { return; }
    gate.hidden = false;

    function mark() {
      return window.ZSLOGO
        ? window.ZSLOGO.monogram({ size: 64, decorative: true })
        : '';
    }

    function paintIntro(cap) {
      if (!cap.ok) {
        /* Say exactly what is wrong and what to do instead. A phone is told
           to use Bluetooth, which is what a phone is for; a desktop browser
           without Web Serial is told which ones have it. */
        gate.innerHTML =
          '<div class="zs-gate-mark">' + mark() + '</div>' +
          '<h2>' + (cap.reason === 'phone'
                    ? 'USB is for the computer app'
                    : 'This browser can’t use USB') + '</h2>' +
          '<p class="zs-gate-why">' + cap.message + '</p>' +
          '<p class="zs-gate-foot">The ZeroStick Core connects to this app over ' +
          'its USB cable on a computer, and over Bluetooth from a phone.</p>' +
          (cap.reason === 'phone'
            ? '<button class="zs-gate-alt" id="usbble">Connect over Bluetooth instead</button>'
            : '');
        var alt = document.getElementById('usbble');
        if (alt) {
          alt.addEventListener('click', function () {
            location.href = 'phone.html?ble=1';
          });
        }
        return;
      }
      gate.innerHTML =
        '<div class="zs-gate-mark">' + mark() + '</div>' +
        '<h2>Connect your ZeroStick Core</h2>' +
        '<p>Plug the Core into this computer with its USB cable. Your ' +
        'computer will ask which port to use; choose the ' +
        '<b>ZeroStick Core</b>.</p>' +
        '<button class="zs-gate-btn" id="usbgo">Connect over USB</button>' +
        '<p class="zs-gate-foot" id="usbnote"></p>';
      document.getElementById('usbgo').addEventListener('click', go);
    }

    function go() {
      var btn = document.getElementById('usbgo');
      var note = document.getElementById('usbnote');
      btn.disabled = true;
      btn.textContent = 'Looking…';
      note.textContent = '';
      window.ZSSERIAL.connect().then(connected).catch(function (e) {
        btn.disabled = false;
        btn.textContent = 'Try again';
        /* ZSSERIAL.explain turns the browser's developer-facing errors into
           something worth reading. Cancelling the chooser is not a failure
           and must not be dressed up as one. */
        note.textContent = window.ZSSERIAL.explain(e);
      });
    }

    function connected(info) {
      gate.hidden = true;
      try {
        if (window.PHONE && window.PHONE.toast) {
          window.PHONE.toast('Connected to ' + (info.name || 'your ZeroStick'));
        }
      } catch (e) { /* cosmetic */ }
      /* Re-read the device NOW rather than at the next 8 s poll: the app
         booted against the shim's "unreachable" answers, and the product
         gate, the title and the config list all follow /status. */
      try {
        if (window.ZS && window.ZS.refreshStatus) {
          window.ZS.refreshStatus().then(function () {
            if (window.ZS.reloadProfiles) { window.ZS.reloadProfiles(); }
          });
        }
      } catch (e2) { /* the poll gets there within 8 s regardless */ }
    }

    /* NO CHOOSER AFTER THE FIRST TIME (2026-10-10 overhaul, audit M34): a
       port this page was granted before is opened again with no click, at
       launch, a moment after the link drops (a Core reset), and when the
       cable goes back in (zs-serial.js reconnect / watch). The gate stays up
       meanwhile, so a failure leaves the ordinary Connect button. */
    function autoConnect() {
      window.ZSSERIAL.reconnect().then(function (info) {
        if (info) { connected(info); }
      }, function (e) {
        var note = document.getElementById('usbnote');
        if (note && !gate.hidden) { note.textContent = window.ZSSERIAL.explain(e); }
      });
    }
    if (window.ZSSERIAL.reconnect) {
      window.ZSSERIAL.watch(connected, function () { /* the Connect button is there */ });
    }

    /* If the cable comes out later, put the gate back rather than letting
       every control quietly fail: a Core that has gone is exactly the thing
       the user needs told about. */
    window.ZSSERIAL.on('status', function (s) {
      if (!s.connected && gate.hidden) {
        paintIntro(window.ZSSERIAL.supported());
        gate.hidden = false;
        if (window.ZSSERIAL.reconnect) { setTimeout(autoConnect, 1500); }
      }
    });

    paintIntro(window.ZSSERIAL.supported());
    if (window.ZSSERIAL.reconnect) { autoConnect(); }
  })();
</script>
<script>
  /* ── brand + motion wiring ─────────────────────────────────────────────
     Everything here is decoration over behaviour that already worked. It is
     wrapped so a failure degrades to "the old look" instead of a dead app —
     an accessibility device that will not open because a logo threw is a far
     worse outcome than an unstyled one. */
  (function () {
    'use strict';

    /* The mark, as vector. Height-led so it sits on the appbar baseline. */
    try {
      if (window.ZSLOGO) {
        /* The header mark is the owner's own artwork now (D185: two
           pictures, one per theme, in the app bar), so the vector lockup no
           longer overwrites it; ZSLOGO still draws the favicon below. */
        var ico = document.querySelector('link[rel="icon"]');
        if (!ico) {
          ico = document.createElement('link');
          ico.rel = 'icon';
          document.head.appendChild(ico);
        }
        ico.href = window.ZSLOGO.faviconURI();
      }
    } catch (e) { /* the <img> fallback is still in the appbar */ }

    /* Springs. attachSheet/attachPanel observe the `open` class rather than
       replacing sheetOpen()/sheetClose(), so every existing call site — here,
       in phone-sections.js and in smart-actions.js — keeps working untouched
       and simply becomes gesture-driven.

       onDismiss/onBack matter: a swipe must run the SAME close path as the
       button, or the sheet's onClose callbacks never fire and the screen
       behind it never learns it needs to refresh. */
    try {
      if (window.ZSM) {
        var wrap = document.getElementById('sheetwrap');
        if (wrap) {
          window.ZSM.attachSheet(wrap, {
            onDismiss: function () {
              /* PHONE.sheetClose is the app's own close; it pops the callback
                 stack and fixes aria-hidden. Fall back to the class only if
                 the app has not exposed it. */
              if (window.PHONE && window.PHONE.sheetClose) { window.PHONE.sheetClose(); }
              else { wrap.classList.remove('open'); wrap.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true'); }
            }
          });
        }

        var ed = document.getElementById('editor');
        if (ed) {
          window.ZSM.attachPanel(ed, {
            onBack: function () {
              var b = document.getElementById('edback');
              if (b) { b.click(); } else { ed.classList.remove('open'); }
            }
          });
        }
        var det = document.getElementById('detail');
        if (det) {
          window.ZSM.attachPanel(det, {
            onBack: function () {
              var b = document.getElementById('dtback');
              if (b) { b.click(); } else { det.classList.remove('open'); }
            }
          });
        }
        var t = document.querySelector('.toast');
        if (t) { window.ZSM.attachToast(t); }

        window.ZSM.pressable(document);
      }
    } catch (e2) { /* CSS transitions still cover open/close */ }
  })();
</script>
